diff --git a/docs/j604.md b/docs/j604.md
new file mode 100644
index 00000000..95d4ee62
--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/j604.md
@@ -0,0 +1,61 @@
+# J604 (MacBook Pro 14-inch, M4, Mac16,1) on the T8132 handoff paths
+
+Status: **draft, not booted.** Hardware tested on: NOT YET.
+
+The J713 handoff paths (aurora-silicon/m1n1 #35-#48, see `docs/j713.md`)
+were qualified on the J713 only. `src/t8132_board.h` admits another T8132
+board **per path**, and only where the values that path depends on are shown
+from source to match the J713. A path that is not admitted behaves as on any
+unqualified M4 board: it is skipped or refused (fails closed). Every admitted
+path also checks its live ADT values at boot and refuses on a mismatch.
+
+## Evidence
+
+The source is Apple's template device trees from the restore IPSWs (Mac16,1 and
+Mac16,12): macOS 27.2 **26B123**, which is the J604's macOS / system
+firmware, and macOS 26.6.2 **25G83**, which is the official M4 stub firmware.
+They were fetched on 2026-11-04 from Apple's public restore images. Only the
+values listed below are used here; no device tree data is included in this
+tree. Templates carry static topology and register windows.
+iBoot fills runtime values at boot (memory map, board-id, carveouts,
+firmware segments), so those values are **TO-VERIFY** on a fresh read-only
+27.2 read-only capture of the J604 (pending).
+
+The register windows below are absolute CPU addresses. They were decoded with
+the arm-io `ranges` and compared J604 against J713 at both 25G83 and 26B123
+(with local ADT decoding scripts).
+Phandle numbering is ignored.
+
+| Path (`T8132_PATH_*`) | Values the path depends on | J604 vs J713 (template) | J604 status |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| STORAGE (ANS adoption for U-Boot, heap/high-water, lifecycle) | ans reg windows incl. 0x485cc0000/0x60000 and 0x4c5cc0000/0x10000. m1n1 does not read ans `msp-bfh-params` or `tunable-table-bundle`, which differ. | regs SAME | admitted; memory extents are runtime, checked by the code |
+| DISPLAY (DCP firmware mappings, inherited display DART) | dart-disp0 0x30a300000/0xc000 + 0x30a314000/0x4000, mapper-disp0-piodma reg 0x10 (stream 16), dart-dcp 0x30a340000/0x20000, dcp 0x30ae00000/0x88000 | SAME. dcp differs only in `audio` and `hdcp-parent` (phandles). disp0 panel properties differ (dot-pitch, supports-apt). | admitted; segment list and UUID are runtime and validated |
+| GPU (G16 handoff) | sgx 0x300000000/0x4000000, gfx-asc 0x302600000/0x88000, gfx-asc interrupts 994/993/996/995 (compared on the whole node) | SAME (gfx-asc identical). sgx differs only in `agx-pmu-loops-unsupported` and `gpu-turbo-controller-input`, which m1n1 does not read. | admitted; the code re-checks these values against the live ADT |
+| DAPF (mtp/pmp/isp consumers requested by the kernel DT) | dart-mtp, dart-pmp, dart-isp nodes incl. `dapf-instance-0` | SAME | admitted |
+| AOP (AOP DAPF bank, firmware segments, windows) | dart-aop SAME; **aop/iop-aop-nub `aop-target` = 0 on J604, 1 on J713** | DIFF | **not admitted, TO-VERIFY**: what aop-target selects, and whether dart-aop DAPF instance 1 applies |
+| ATC_USB3 (T8132 USB3 tunables copied from the ADT) | atc-phy0/1 nodes; tunables are read from the live ADT | atc-phy0/1 SAME; J604 also has atc-phy2 (third USB-C port) | admitted; the extra port is described by the kernel DT |
+| PCIE_RADIO (SMC gP13 rail, SID16 bypass, PCIe disable on failure) | amfm `function-reg_on` = SMC GPIO `gP13`, action `pKW4`, argument 0x00800000 (`SMC_J713_RADIO_REG_ON_TEMPLATE`); dart-apcie0 0x490000000/0x20000; apcie 0x1cb0000000/0x10000000 ECAM | SAME (only the SMC phandle differs). J604 also has apcie pci-bridge2/pcie-sdreader (`sd_pwr_en` = gP19, argument 0) and dart-apcie2. pci-bridge0 `default-apcie-options` differs, but m1n1 does not read it. | admitted for the radio; see the risk below |
+
+CPU topology (used by `cpu_start_bit()`): cpu0-5 are E cores, cluster-id 0,
+reg 0x0-0x5; cpu6-9 are P cores, cluster-id 1, reg 0x100-0x103. This is
+identical on J604 and J713 at 26B123. The boot CPU is a runtime value
+(TO-VERIFY).
+
+## TO-VERIFY on the fresh 27.2 J604 capture
+
+1. `/chosen` board-id, dram-base/dram-size, and the carveout memory map (STORAGE).
+2. DCP `segment-ranges` and the firmware UUID (DISPLAY). The code validates both and refuses on a mismatch.
+3. sgx `gpu-region`, `gfx-shared-region`, `gfx-handoff`, `gfx-shared-l2-region`, and the gfx-asc segments (GPU).
+4. aop/iop-aop-nub `aop-target` and `segment-ranges`, and the dart-aop DAPF instances (AOP; held back until then).
+5. The boot CPU, and the PMGR CPU_START status before and after secondary start (logged by m1n1 around each CPU start).
+6. The kernel DT compatible for J604 (`apple,j604`) and its PCIe port description (kernel device tree).
+
+## Known risk: J604 SD reader port
+
+`pcie_prepare_fdt()` powers each enabled T8132 port that carries `pwren-gpios`,
+and accepts only the gP13 radio rail. If the J604 kernel DT gives the SD reader
+port a `pwren-gpios` (gP19), preparation fails, and kboot disables every
+`apple,t8132-pcie` host: the radio and the SD reader together. That is fail
+closed, but costs Wi-Fi. Either the kernel DT leaves the SD reader rail to
+Linux, or the SD rail gets its own source-verified entry. Both are kernel-side
+decisions, out of scope here.
